#!/bin/bash
# land — merge a PR and put it in production, as ONE action.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. On 18 Sep 2026 four PRs were merged and nothing was deployed.
# She opened the app to look at a redesign that had landed on `main` an hour
# earlier and saw the old page. Her words: "i dont see a home page redesign" and
# then "how can we fucking fix this so that you merge and deploy at the same
# time". The failure was never permission — merging unprompted was already the
# standing rule. The failure was that merge and deploy were two steps, and the
# second one waited on somebody remembering it.
#
# A rule in CLAUDE.md telling the next agent to remember would be a wish. This is
# the rule as code: you cannot run the merge half without the deploy half.
#
#   land            # the PR for the current branch
#   land 96         # that PR number
#   land 96 --owner="<her words>"  # HER bypass (21 Nov 2026): skips the review, click-through and patch
#                      # sign-offs; CI, the safety checks, deploy and smoke still run. Only when SHE says ship it.
#                      # Once green, `ENG-CHECK DUE <repo>#<pr> <sha>` goes into ~/Work/tasks/post-land-checks.md (same-day review),
#                      # as it does for EVERY land with no fresh reviewer's PASS (Fast PRs: the day's batched check).
#   land --check 96    # READ-ONLY: every description gate at once; prints ALL that is missing, each with the line to
#                      # add from pr-body-template.md. exit 0 nothing to fix | 1 the list | 75 GitHub did not answer.
#   land 96 --run-e2e  # that PR, and run the full e2e suite on main before production whatever its size
#   land --promote [<repo>] [--run-e2e]   # ship the newest e2e-green main commit to production
#
# DONE MEANS CHECKED (her order 24 Nov 2026, after Noelle landed green and acted on 0 of 11 decisions: "u said
# nothing yesterday"). Every PR states `Outcome:` and `## Not built`, or land stops by name. ONE file carries the
# exact form of every line land can ask for: pr-body-template.md, beside this script. A description that lacks
# several is told ALL of them in one run, each with the line to add, then stopped once: NAMED STOP
# [OUTCOME_UNDECLARED], [NOT_BUILT_UNDECLARED], [FORMS_UNDECLARED] and the rest of LAND_BODY_CODES; a check that is not
# runnable with no shell and no logins (`outcome-read --explain` says why) → NAMED STOP [OUTCOME_CHECK_NOT_READ_ONLY]. land prints Not built
# word for word, and on a green land with a real Outcome writes `OUTCOME-CHECK DUE <repo>#<pr> <sha> — task:<slug>
# by:<day> …` into ~/Work/tasks/post-land-checks.md; post-land-check runs that check after its by-day and only its PASS makes the
# work "working" (land-after's sheet says LANDED-UNCHECKED until then, DONE after). Her bypass is never blocked: it
# records `Outcome: UNDECLARED (owner bypass)` and writes a DUE line whose check the fresh Claude infers.
#
# MERGE QUEUE (15 Nov 2026): before merging, a post-merge e2e or deploy run still going on main is
# waited for (bounded), never merged over and cancelled; another PR landed on the repo in the last 30
# minutes prints "batch fixes into one round".
#
# A SMALL change ships to production on the fast check alone; a LARGE one (or --run-e2e) runs the
# full e2e suite on main first and ships only on green. "Large" is defined once, in the `large`
# block of this file. A suite already known red on main blocks a small change (E2E_KNOWN_RED).
#
# It refuses rather than guesses: not green, head moved, main red -> it stops and
# says which, and it does NOT deploy. A red main after a merge is the case this
# repo has actually hit (PR #16 in boss-os passed on its head and the merge
# commit went red, already deployed by then), so main is watched to a terminal
# state every single time, not sampled once.
#
# BUILD FIRST, TEST IN BATCHES (26 Sep 2026). A repo whose route names a full e2e workflow
# (E2E_WF) no longer runs that suite per merge: the merge gate is the fast check, and the browser
# suites run nightly + on dispatch. So for those repos `land <pr>` is: fast check green -> merge ->
# main's FAST run green -> deploy STAGING from the merge sha -> production as the plan block decides
# (SMALL CHANGES SHIP ON THE FAST CHECK, below). `land --promote <repo>` ships the newest main
# commit whose e2e run passed and that is newer than what production runs. Production's record is the GitHub Deployments API (environment
# "production", one deployment per land, `gh api repos/../deployments`), written by this script on
# every production deploy of an e2e-routed repo and read back by --promote; nothing is written to
# the git graph. Both deploys run from a clean detached worktree at exactly that sha, never the
# working tree. `--run-e2e` makes --promote dispatch the e2e workflow on main's head and wait for it
# (ceiling 1.5x the last completed run) when no green run is newer than production. A repo whose
# origin/main carries .github/workflows/promote.yml promotes itself on a green e2e: --promote waits
# for any in-flight promote run (bounded) before reading production, never deploys on top of one.
# Repos with no E2E_WF on their route are unchanged: merge -> production, as before.
#
# ON DEMAND ONLY, AND AFTER A LARGE CHANGE (owner, 2 Oct 2026). The e2e workflows no longer run on
# any schedule (`workflow_dispatch` only), so nothing would ever run them after a merge unless a
# person remembered. That is Rule 0 with a person in the loop. So on an E2E_WF route, when the
# merged sha has no green run, `land <pr>` measures the PR (the `large` block below: lines, files,
# a schema or browser-test change, a UI surface touched) and, when it is LARGE, dispatches the e2e
# workflow on main itself, waits for it (bounded), and ships production only on green — printing
# WHY it decided the change was large, and recording that reason on the GitHub Deployment.
# `land <pr> --run-e2e` forces the suite for any size. A red run is a NAMED STOP: staging runs the
# sha, production is untouched.
#
# SMALL CHANGES SHIP ON THE FAST CHECK (owner, 2 Oct 2026, asked and answered). With the suites on
# demand only, a small change used to print WAITING and hold production for a green e2e on its sha
# that nothing would ever run: six repos sat on staging behind CI/docs/test-only changes. Her
# ruling: a SMALL change ships to production on the fast check alone (typecheck, unit, validators);
# e2e gates production only after a LARGE change or when asked (--run-e2e). So on an E2E_WF route:
#   · SMALL  -> main's fast check green -> production from the merge sha, printing and recording
#               "small change: N lines, M files; shipped on the fast check, e2e on demand".
#   · LARGE  -> unchanged: the suite runs on main's head, production only on green.
#   · EVERY CHANGE PRODUCTION HAS NOT SEEN IS JUDGED, not only this PR: each commit between what
#     production runs (its GitHub Deployment record) and the sha about to ship is measured by the
#     same `large` block. One that is large and still unproven (its suite never dispatched, was
#     cancelled, or it was merged without land) makes this land run the suite — a large change
#     never rides out on the small one behind it. Production unreadable, or not an ancestor: large.
#   · KNOWN RED BLOCKS. The newest run of the e2e workflow on main that reached a verdict
#     (cancelled and skipped runs are not verdicts: e2e.yml cancels a superseded dispatch by
#     design) must be success, or there must be none at all. Anything else — failure, timed_out,
#     startup_failure — is a suite KNOWN RED with no green run after it, and a small change is not
#     shipped past it: NAMED STOP [E2E_KNOWN_RED], staging runs the sha, production untouched; fix
#     main and `land <pr> --run-e2e` ships it on a green suite. A red run on the exact sha blocks
#     likewise. A history that cannot be read is not "not red": exit 75, production untouched.
# A self-deploying repo whose production moves through its own workflow (PROMOTE_VIA on the route:
# secondaries, founder-dilution-dashboard, justbeingmercedes) follows the same plan: land confirms
# the staging build, then dispatches that workflow with the sha and the reason; the workflow checks
# the fast check and the known-red rule itself before it moves production, and records the
# GitHub Deployment.
set -uo pipefail
# ~/bin/gh (the shared allowance) never sleeps past the whole land's own wait (LAND_RATE_WAIT, 65 min):
# a longer wait comes back as "rate limit: …" and land's own rate-limit handling decides. A caller's
# tighter deadline (land-after's) is kept.
export GH_SHARE_UNTIL="${GH_SHARE_UNTIL:-$(( $(date +%s) + ${LAND_RATE_WAIT:-3900} ))}"

# RUN FROM A COPY. bash reads a script incrementally, so editing this file while a `land` is in
# flight corrupts that run: on 21 Sep 2026 an edit landed mid-run and a live `land 152` died with
# "syntax error near unexpected token `else'" at a line it had not reached when it started. The
# first thing this script does is copy itself somewhere immutable and re-exec from there.
if [ -z "${LAND_RUNNING_FROM_COPY:-}" ]; then
  _copy="$(mktemp -t land.XXXXXX)" || exit 1
  cat "$0" > "$_copy" && chmod +x "$_copy"
  LAND_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)" LAND_RUNNING_FROM_COPY=1 exec bash "$_copy" "$@"
fi

say() { printf '\n\033[1m%s\033[0m\n' "$*"; }
# EVERY STOP SAYS THREE THINGS (her yes 25 Nov 2026, the 5 lines; built 27 Nov 2026): what was expected, what
# happened, and where to look; it tells a test that failed apart from a check that never ran; and when the same PR
# stopped before, it says what was tried then, so a second attempt does not repeat the first. Each stage of land
# says what it expects by setting EXPECT="<what>" (and LOOK="<where to look>" when it knows better); `die "<what happened>"` prints all three, so no
# stop can leave a part out (tests/test-land-stop-shape.sh reads every stop in tests/test-land-flow.sh's runs).
EXPECT="land to read its arguments and the repo it was started in"; LOOK=""
look_here() { # where to look, most specific first: the stage's own pointer, the run land watched, the PR's checks
  if [ -n "$LOOK" ]; then printf '%s' "$LOOK"; return; fi
  local rid="${DRID:-${RID:-}}"
  if [ -n "$rid" ]; then printf 'gh run view %s --log-failed' "$rid"
  elif [ -n "${PR:-}" ]; then printf 'gh pr checks %s; gh pr view %s' "$PR" "$PR"
  else printf 'the output above'; fi
  [ -z "${LAND_TASK:-}" ] || printf '; the land log ~/Work/tasks/logs/%s-%s.log' "$LAND_TASK" "${PR:-}"
}
STOPS_DIR="${LAND_STOPS_DIR:-$HOME/.cache/land/stops}"
tried_before() { # $1 what happened now -> prints the last stop of this PR (if any), then records this one
  [ -n "${PR:-}" ] || return 0
  local f; f="$STOPS_DIR/$(printf '%s' "${LAND_REPO_KEY:-$(git config --get remote.origin.url 2>/dev/null || pwd)}#$PR" | tr -c 'A-Za-z0-9._-' '_')"
  if [ -s "$f" ]; then
    local when was; IFS=$'\t' read -r when was < "$f"
    if [ "$was" = "$(printf '%s' "$1" | tr '\t\n' '  ' | cut -c1-400)" ]; then
      printf '  tried before:  %s, and it stopped the SAME way. Running land again unchanged will stop here again: change what is named above first.\n' "$when"
    else
      printf '  tried before:  %s, it stopped with: %s\n' "$when" "$(printf '%s' "$was" | cut -c1-240)"
    fi
  fi
  mkdir -p "$STOPS_DIR" 2>/dev/null && printf '%s\t%s\n' "$(TZ=America/Chicago date '+%d %b %H:%M CT')" "$(printf '%s' "$1" | tr '\t\n' '  ' | cut -c1-400)" > "$f" 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}
die() {
  printf '\n\033[31mSTOPPED: %s\033[0m\n' "$*"
  printf '  expected:      %s\n  what happened: %s\n  where to look: %s\n' "$EXPECT" "$(printf '%s' "$*" | sed -E 's/^NAMED STOP \[[A-Z_]+\] //' | cut -c1-300)" "$(look_here)"
  tried_before "$*"
  exit 1
}

MODE="land"; PR=""; TARGET=""; RUN_E2E=""; OWNER_WORDS=""
for a in "$@"; do
  case "$a" in
    --promote) MODE="promote" ;;
    --check) MODE="check" ;;
    --run-e2e) RUN_E2E=1 ;;
    --owner=*) OWNER_WORDS="${a#--owner=}" ;;
    -h|--help) sed -n '2,40p' "$0" | sed 's/^# \{0,1\}//'; exit 0 ;;
    --*) die "unknown flag $a (land [<pr>] [--owner="<her words>"] | land --check [<pr>] | land --promote [<repo>] [--run-e2e])" ;;
    *) if [ "$MODE" = promote ]; then TARGET="$a"; else PR="$a"; fi ;;
  esac
done
[ "$MODE" != check ] || { [ -z "$OWNER_WORDS" ] && [ -z "$RUN_E2E" ]; } || die "land --check only reads: it takes a PR number and nothing else (--owner writes her bypass onto the PR; give it to land itself)"
if [ -n "$TARGET" ]; then
  case "$TARGET" in */*|.*) : ;; *) [ -d "$TARGET" ] || TARGET="$HOME/GitHub/$TARGET" ;; esac
  [ -d "$TARGET" ] || die "no such repo directory: $TARGET"
  cd "$TARGET" || die "cannot enter $TARGET"
fi
REPO_DIR="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)" || { echo "not in a git repo"; exit 1; }
cd "$REPO_DIR" || exit 1
# A worktree is named after its branch dir, not the repo. Ask git for the
# repo's own name (the common dir) so `land` from a worktree routes correctly.
# --- name begin
NAME="$(basename "$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir | sed 's#/\.git$##')")"
# The checkout's folder is not always the repo's name (boss-os lives at ~/.boss-os/runtime, 4 Nov
# 2026: "runtime has no deploy route"): the origin remote's name wins when there is one.
# Only a GitHub remote names the repo (a local or test remote is a path, not a name).
REMOTE_URL="$(git remote get-url origin 2>/dev/null || true)"
case "$REMOTE_URL" in
  *github.com[:/]*) REMOTE_NAME="$(printf '%s' "$REMOTE_URL" | sed -E 's#\.git$##; s#.*/##')"; [ -n "$REMOTE_NAME" ] && NAME="$REMOTE_NAME" ;;
esac
# Parked repos are never landed (her ruling 18 Nov 2026: "courtscope is parked we should skip
# any activity on this repo going forward"). The list is Boss OS maps/repos.json category "parked".
if python3 -c 'import json,sys
d=json.load(open(sys.argv[1])); rows=d if isinstance(d,list) else d.get("rows",d.get("repos",[]))
sys.exit(0 if any(r.get("repo")==sys.argv[2] and r.get("category")=="parked" for r in rows) else 1)' \
  "${BOSS_OS_REPO:-$HOME/GitHub/boss-os}/src/shared/boss/maps/repos.json" "$NAME" 2>/dev/null; then
  echo "STOPPED: $NAME is parked (Boss OS repos.json). No work lands on a parked repo until she un-parks it."; exit 1
fi
# --- name end

# The 7403 trap. `npm run deploy:production` in west-peek-os does NOT default the
# account id the way boss-os's script does, so in any shell that lacks it — a
# background job, a fresh login, a launchd context — the very first step fails
# with "The given account is not valid or is not authorized to access this
# service [code: 7403]". That reads like a revoked token and is not one. It cost
# a deploy on 18 Sep 2026: main was green, the merge was done, and production
# silently stayed on the old build. Set it here so no caller has to know.
export CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID="${CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID:-8d147e242033699dd37c6f5a451f48d2}"

# --- verify helpers begin (tests/test-land-flow.sh drives the whole script through them) ---
# COULD NOT CHECK IS NOT GREEN, AND IT IS NOT RED EITHER. 26 Sep 2026, in one sweep: how-we-know
# #130's head lookup got "HTTP 401: Requires authentication", this script printed "green, head ,
# MERGEABLE/CLEAN" with an EMPTY head and merged; and horse-legal-guide-velocity #35 got a "TLS
# handshake timeout" on its first call and was refused as "PR #35 is not green" — a green PR left
# unmerged on a network blip. Both are the same defect: an unanswered question read as an answer.
# So every verification read goes through gh_read, which retries a failed read (bounded: 3
# attempts, 2s then 4s), and a read that never answers stops with exit 75 (EX_TEMPFAIL) and the
# words "could not verify", distinct from a real refusal (exit 1), so a caller can try again.
LAND_RC_UNVERIFIED=75
unverified() {
  printf '\n\033[31mSTOPPED: could not verify (%s) — %s. Nothing was merged by this step; land again when GitHub answers.\033[0m\n' "$1" "$2"
  unanswered_parts "$1"
  exit "$LAND_RC_UNVERIFIED"
}
unanswered_parts() { # $1 what did not answer -> the three parts of a could-not-verify stop (never a failed test)
  printf '  expected:      %s\n  what happened: %s did not answer in time, so nothing was checked: this is not a failed test\n  where to look: %s\n' "$EXPECT" "${1:-GitHub}" "$(look_here)"
}
# gh_read runs inside $( ), so what went wrong is left in a file, not a variable the subshell loses.
GH_ERR_FILE="$(mktemp -t land-gh-err.XXXXXX)" || exit 1
RATE_UNTIL_FILE="$(mktemp -t land-rate-until.XXXXXX)" || { rm -f "$GH_ERR_FILE"; exit 1; } # rate_wait's deadline, below
gh_err() { cat "$GH_ERR_FILE" 2>/dev/null; }
land_exit() { # $1 exit code. A land that ended green with no fresh reviewer's PASS (her bypass, or a Fast PR) notes its same-day engineering check; a landed launch lists its repo.
  local rc="$1"
  if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ -n "${LAND_ENG_DUE:-}" ]; then note_eng_check "$LAND_ENG_DUE" || rc="$LAND_RC_ENG_CHECK_NOT_NOTED"; fi
  if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ -n "${FORMS_DUE:-}" ]; then note_form_proof "$FORMS_DUE" || rc="$LAND_RC_ENG_CHECK_NOT_NOTED"; fi
  if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ -n "${OUTCOME_DUE:-}" ]; then note_outcome_check "$OUTCOME_DUE" || rc="$LAND_RC_ENG_CHECK_NOT_NOTED"; fi
  if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ -n "${LIVE_ADD:-}" ]; then live_add "$LIVE_ADD" "$PR" || rc="$LAND_RC_LIVE_NOT_ADDED"; fi
  rm -f "$GH_ERR_FILE" "$RATE_UNTIL_FILE"
  # A TERM, HUP or Ctrl-C mid-deploy leaves no temp worktree or step file (deploy_cleanup, in deploy_at).
  if declare -F deploy_cleanup >/dev/null; then deploy_cleanup; fi
  exit "$rc"
}
trap 'land_exit $?' EXIT # bash runs it on TERM, HUP and INT too (tests/test-land-local-ship.sh)
gh_kind() { # stderr text -> network | auth | api
  # A GitHub 5xx is "did not answer", never a refusal. 30 Oct 2026: during a GitHub incident
  # the merge call of three green PRs (how-we-know#256, local-guides-generator#140,
  # sprylabs-hpc-site#169) got 'GraphQL: Something went wrong while executing your query',
  # which fell through to `api`, so land died rc=1 and the sweep read it as a refusal
  # instead of exit 75 and landing again after a pause.
  if grep -qiE 'timeout|timed out|connection (reset|refused)|no such host|dial tcp|EOF|TLS|i/o|network is unreachable|HTTP 5[0-9][0-9]|temporarily unavailable|Something went wrong while executing your query|Internal Server Error|Bad Gateway|Service Unavailable|Gateway Time-?out' <<<"$1"; then echo network
  elif is_rate_limit "$1"; then echo "rate limit"
  elif grep -qiE 'HTTP 401|HTTP 403|authentication|gh auth login' <<<"$1"; then echo auth
  else echo api; fi
}
# A SPENT ALLOWANCE IS A WAIT, NOT A FAILURE (22 Nov 2026: #134's land chain died at 09:35 CT on
# "API rate limit exceeded", read as "could not verify (auth)"). land sleeps to GitHub's reset (rate_reset_in, in
# the gates block: land-after's patient() uses the same one) and asks again, within LAND_RATE_WAIT for the WHOLE
# land (65 min: an hourly allowance always fits). The deadline lives in a file, like GH_ERR_FILE: gh_read runs in
# $( ), and a deadline kept in a variable was a fresh 65 min for every read (review F3). Past it: "could not
# verify (rate limit)", exit 75.
rate_wait() { # $1 gh's error text -> rc 0 after sleeping to the reset | 1 not a rate limit, or past the land's bound
  is_rate_limit "$1" || return 1
  local until left
  until="$(cat "$RATE_UNTIL_FILE" 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ "$until" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || { until=$(( $(date +%s) + ${LAND_RATE_WAIT:-3900} )); printf '%s' "$until" > "$RATE_UNTIL_FILE"; }
  left="$(rate_reset_in)"
  [ $(( $(date +%s) + left )) -le "$until" ] || return 1
  echo "     GitHub's allowance is spent; waiting ${left}s for its reset, then asking again" >&2
  sleep "$left"
}
gh_read() { # $1 validation regex for stdout; rest: gh args. Prints stdout; 0 = valid answer, 1 = none after retries
  local want="$1" out err_f GH_ERR try=1 backoff="${LAND_RETRY_SECS:-2}"; shift
  err_f="$(mktemp)"
  while :; do
    out="$(gh "$@" 2>"$err_f")"
    if [ -n "$out" ] && grep -qE "$want" <<<"$out"; then rm -f "$err_f"; printf '%s\n' "$out"; return 0; fi
    GH_ERR="$(head -3 "$err_f" | paste -sd' ' -)"; [ -n "$GH_ERR" ] || GH_ERR="empty answer from gh $1 $2"
    printf '%s' "$GH_ERR" > "$GH_ERR_FILE"
    rate_wait "$GH_ERR" && continue
    [ "$try" -ge 3 ] && { rm -f "$err_f"; return 1; }
    echo "     gh $1 $2 did not answer ($GH_ERR) — attempt $try of 3, again in ${backoff}s" >&2
    sleep "$backoff"; try=$((try + 1)); backoff=$((backoff * 2))
  done
}
not_green() { # $1 the PR's checks JSON -> "<name>: <bucket>" per check that is not pass or skipping ("" = all green)
  jq -r 'if length == 0 then "no checks reported" else (.[] | select(.bucket != "pass" and .bucket != "skipping") | "\(.name): \(.bucket)") end' <<<"$1"
}
merge_clean() { # sets STATE; dies unless PR $PR is MERGEABLE and CLEAN|HAS_HOOKS|UNSTABLE (75 when unread)
  # UNKNOWN is GitHub still computing — it answers that for a minute after anything merges to main
  # (19 Sep: #132 stopped as UNKNOWN/UNKNOWN seconds after #135 landed, and was clean). Wait it out.
  for _ in $(seq 1 12); do
    STATE="$(gh_read '^[A-Z_]+/[A-Z_]+$' pr view "$PR" --json mergeable,mergeStateStatus -q '.mergeable+"/"+.mergeStateStatus')" \
      || unverified "$(gh_kind "$(gh_err)")" "PR #$PR's merge state could not be read: $(gh_err)"
    [ "${STATE%%/*}" = "UNKNOWN" ] || break
    sleep 10
  done
  [ "${STATE%%/*}" = "MERGEABLE" ] || die "PR #$PR is $STATE — resolve conflicts BY HAND, never with a union script"
  case "${STATE##*/}" in
    CLEAN|HAS_HOOKS|UNSTABLE) : ;;   # UNSTABLE is a failing NON-required check; the bucket scan already refused a real failure
    *) die "PR #$PR merge state is ${STATE##*/} — not landing" ;;
  esac
}
# A MERGE RACE IS NOT A FAILURE (22 Nov 2026, boss-os#518: "Base branch was modified. Review and try the merge
# again" because another PR merged a second earlier). Before each new try the PR is verified again from GitHub:
# every check green, the head still the one verified, the merge state clean. Red, a new head or a dirty state
# stops (exit 1); an unread answer is "could not verify" (75). The try itself is the same pinned merge: never
# --admin, never --auto, never past branch protection.
still_ready() { # after a race: rc 0 when PR $PR is still green, at $HEAD and clean; dies otherwise
  local c ng h
  c="$(gh_read '^\[' pr checks "$PR" --json name,bucket,link)" \
    || unverified "$(gh_kind "$(gh_err)")" "main moved under the merge, and PR #$PR's checks could not be read again: $(gh_err)"
  ng="$(not_green "$c")"
  [ -z "$ng" ] || die "main moved under the merge, and PR #$PR is no longer green — $(tr '\n' ';' <<<"$ng"). Nothing was merged."
  h="$(gh_read '^[0-9a-f]{40}$' pr view "$PR" --json headRefOid -q .headRefOid)" \
    || unverified "$(gh_kind "$(gh_err)")" "main moved under the merge, and PR #$PR's head could not be read again: $(gh_err)"
  [ "$h" = "$HEAD" ] || die "main moved under the merge, and PR #$PR's head moved from ${HEAD:0:7} to ${h:0:7}. Nothing was merged: land $PR again so the new head is verified."
  merge_clean
}
# --- verify helpers end ---

# How this repo reaches production. westpeek-live deploys itself through its own GitHub workflow
# (DEPLOY_WF) on green main — Workers Builds only uploads it — and land waits for that run; the
# two Worker repos deploy only when a person runs the script.
# Every West Peek web property the "web property change" lane can target MUST have a row here —
# tests/test-land-routes.sh evals this block verbatim and fails if one is missing.
# A row may also set: STAGING (a staging deploy command, run from the merge sha right after
# main's fast check is green), E2E_WF (the name of the full post-merge browser workflow; set,
# a LARGE change waits for a green run of it; a small one ships on the fast check — see the header),
# SMOKE (a URL that must answer 200 with "ok":true after a production deploy), PROMOTE_VIA (a
# self-deploying repo's own workflow that moves production; land dispatches it with `-f sha=` and
# `-f reason=` instead of running a deploy command) and PROMOTE_WF (the repo workflow that ships
# production by itself on a green e2e — waited for, never raced; default promote.yml, and
# PROMOTE_VIA when that is set) and DEPLOY_WF (the repo workflow file that ships production by
# itself on green main, for a route with no E2E_WF — land finds its run on the merge commit, waits
# for it, bounded, and reports its real conclusion; never races it. Default deploy.yml for a route
# with a DEPLOY command; a self-deploying route names it, or tests/test-land-routes.sh fails when
